detail.abstract:
Purpose – The study aimed to revisit the impact of economic growth on income inequality in South Africa and further to examine the effectiveness of the fiscal policy in achieving the government objective using the data from low- and high-income earners covering the period 1980 to 2022.&nbsp;
<div>
 Design/methodological/approach– To achieve the main objective, this study utilized the nonlinear autoregressive distributed lag (NARDL) bounds since this approach builds the track of the cumulative effects of positive and negative changes in the independent variable separately over time. This enables the model to distinguish between how improvement in economic growth inversely affects the income inequality of low-income earners and high-income earners.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Findings - The study found nonlinear effects between economic growth and income inequality in South Africa where, during high growth, inequality decreased amongst low-income earners but was exacerbated amongst high-income earners. In contrast, lower-income earners suffer the most during periods of economic contraction. The study found a U-shaped relationship for the lower income segment, contradicting the Kuznets hypothesis, while aligning with the inverted U-shape hypothesis for the high-income segment. The study reveals that government intervention through distributional channels significantly impacts inequality in the lower-income segment, while contributing to inequality in the high segment, while progressive tax increases inequality in the lower-income segment and decreases it in the high segment, disproportionately affecting the lower-income segment.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Originality – The contribution of this paper is firstly to examine the growth-inequality relationship and compare the lower-income learner with the high-income earner, while further tracing how these two groups respond to positive and negative growth, and secondly fiscal intervention. This paper proposes that policymakers should tailor interventions: shield low-income earners during downturns with targeted support, reform progressive taxes to avoid burdening the poor, and promote inclusive growth sectors. Efficient redistribution must prevent unintended inequality among higher earners, ensuring balanced benefits across the different income groups for sustainable, equitable development in South Africa.
</div>
